WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.186
Case One assigns timber to building roles.

00:00:03.186 --> 00:00:09.468
Case Two asks how short, straight reclaimed strips can overlap to form a continuous seat and backrest.

00:00:12.564 --> 00:00:18.159
The same source evidence, recorded dimensions and quantities feed the original allocation workflow.

00:00:18.159 --> 00:00:21.436
Each cutting mark belongs to a real parent stock interval.

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:25.987
A source bar can supply several different bench positions.

00:00:25.987 --> 00:00:30.484
Follow the highlighted strip from its cutting interval to its saved location.

00:00:30.484 --> 00:00:33.462
Surplus parts and retained residuals stay separate.

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:39.053
These are straight rectangular members, crosscut to length.

00:00:39.053 --> 00:00:41.921
Their orientation and overlap create the contour.

00:00:41.921 --> 00:00:46.017
There are no curved plywood ribs or contour machining in this version.

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:51.339
The remaining allocation accelerates using one shared stock ledger.

00:00:51.339 --> 00:00:58.202
The complete bench contains seven hundred and sixteen installed strips, cut from one hundred and twenty unique parent bars.

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:04.521
The bench is about six metres long at the recorded illustrative scale.

00:01:04.521 --> 00:01:09.058
Its source-linked appearance is representative, not an exact scanned surface map.

00:01:10.451 --> 00:01:15.032
Four terminal overlaps use a third idealized point to prevent rotation.

00:01:15.032 --> 00:01:17.549
This does not verify fastener capacity.

00:01:18.176 --> 00:01:23.151
The saved R three analysis compares self weight and two one kilonewton load cases.

00:01:23.151 --> 00:01:26.184
Six unanchored levelling bearings remain explicit.

00:01:26.184 --> 00:01:29.824
Fastener capacity and bearing friction are still unverified.
